#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124127 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ee9cbe8-ad40-5155-b42d-1802f36adf27" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Global Extrema of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/id #uuid "137818ca-6c48-5ae1-8788-2015f7890ad5" :question/math-academy-id "e-12460" :question/problem "The graph $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. What are the global maximum points of the function?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1945/Source/Images/5e11926055cd53b797f7b551db802dde.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global maximum of a function is the largest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum.\n- A global maximum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global maximum. There can be zero, one, or many global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global maximum is $2$. The function reaches this value when\n\n$$\n(x,y) = (0,-2)\n$$\n\nso the global maximum point is $(0,-2,2)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1945/Source/Images/03dac4b5188cba2c29ae834a68a2ac47.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124128 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0fb118f-c1cc-51fd-a8cc-c29cf705acfe" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Local Extrema of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/id #uuid "976e4ec4-93f9-57a4-9f2b-2116e09fed22" :question/math-academy-id "e-12461" :question/problem "The graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local minimum points?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1945/Source/Images/6ed81efec74ea191b65f65cf5cbf32bd.png)" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if\n\n$$\nf(x,y) \\geq f(a,b)\n$$\n\nfor all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b)$.\n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch regions exist for the points $A, B$, and $D$. Hence, $A, B$, and $D$ are local minimum points.\n\n**Note**: For the point $D(x_D,y_D,z_D)$, there are points $(x,y)$ in the surrounding regions such that\n\n$$\nf(x_D,y_D) = f(x,y)\n$$\n\nHowever, since the definition of local extrema is non-strict,\n\n$$\nf(x,y) \\geq f(a,b)\n$$\n\n, the point $D$ does satisfy the conditions for a local minimum.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1945/Source/Images/95fe3bf8748bc789ab6a58b3ea36f6e1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124129 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "233eab4c-ab17-53a8-ae03-f6f28ac78922" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "94c54743-ba74-51c5-9c59-777125bb76f6" :question/math-academy-id "e-12465" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(\\mathbf x)$ about $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a\\in\\mathbb R^2$ is given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ are $\\lambda_1 = -1$ and $\\lambda_2 = -3$, what type of point is $\\mathbf{a}$?" :question/worked-solution "The point $\\mathbf{a} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a critical point of the function $f: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ if\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}'(\\mathbf{a}) = \\mathbf{0}^T\n$$\n\nIn such cases, we can classify $\\mathbf{a}$ as follows:\n\n- if $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is positive definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local minimum\n- if $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is negative definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local maximum\n- if $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ about\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{a} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n\n$$\n\ngiven by\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) \\approx f(\\mathbf{a}) + \\mathbf{f}'(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a}) + \\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a}) (\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})$ term, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a}) = \\mathbf{0}^T\n$$\n\nHence, $\\mathbf a$ is a critical point of $f$.\n\nFinally, notice that both the eigenvalues of $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ are negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ_{1} &= -1 < 0 \\\\\nλ_{2} &= -3 < 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is negative definite. Therefore, $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local maximum of $f$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124297 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40d58ee6-1121-5ddc-a96f-1a7fa7a193dd" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Sylvester's Criterion"}] :question/id #uuid "c6938151-ab77-5288-ba08-156dc942ddec" :question/math-academy-id "e-7030" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(\\mathbf x)$ about $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a\\in\\mathbb R^3$ is given by\n$f(\\mathbf{x}) \\approx 2 + \\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})^T A (\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})$,\n\nwhere\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $a_{11} = 1$ and $\\det(A) = -4$\n2. $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite\n3. $\\mathbf{a}$ is a saddle point of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The point $\\mathbf{a} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a critical point of the function $f: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ if\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}'(\\mathbf{a}) = \\mathbf{0}^T\n$$\n\nIn such cases, we can classify $\\mathbf{a}$ as follows:\n\n- if $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is positive definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local minimum\n- if $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is negative definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local maximum\n- if $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\nA = \\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})\n$$\n\nNow, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet $\\Delta_i$ for\n\n$$\ni=1,2,\\ldots,n\n$$\n\ndenote the principal minors of an $n \\times n$ matrix $A$. Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is\n\n- positive-definite if and only if $\\Delta_i > 0$ for all $i=1,2,\\ldots,n$,\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,\\ldots,n$, we have $\\Delta_i < 0$ when $i$ is odd, and $\\Delta_i > 0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $a_{11} = 1$, but\n$det(A)|=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}; = 1 \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}- 1 \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}; = 1(0) - 1(2); =-2$.\n- Statement II is true. Notice the following:\n$Δ_{1}|= a_{11} = 1 > 0; Δ_{2}|= \\mid 1\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}2|= 1 > 0; Δ_{3}|= \\mid A|=-2 < 0$\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite.\n- Statement III is true. Since the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, the critical point $\\mathbf{a}$ is a saddle point of $f$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124730 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ee9cbe8-ad40-5155-b42d-1802f36adf27" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Global Extrema of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cafe3a8a-05a4-508f-8dca-2606a488d835" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121400} {:answer/id #uuid "d2ef5011-54dc-5fde-8431-1461516828ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121401} {:answer/id #uuid "ed1dc233-359e-5d16-ae61-f00ab707027c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121402} {:answer/id #uuid "00831521-2e14-5d3f-9047-43ad6d2c38e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121403} {:answer/id #uuid "09f8baf2-9ef7-5987-bebb-73313c3916e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed1dc233-359e-5d16-ae61-f00ab707027c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121402} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8cc3847-5a4c-5ede-b026-8f6a8bc939db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121399}] :question/id #uuid "043d8ef6-a714-5ba6-9a5f-496901cda0dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-162706" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1945/Source/Images/q-162706.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z = f(x, y)$ is shown above.\nWhat is the global minimum of the function?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124732 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0fb118f-c1cc-51fd-a8cc-c29cf705acfe" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Local Extrema of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d08bb1b-22c1-5994-86ce-697355d9d5b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121412} {:answer/id #uuid "176e41a7-608c-52b7-a36d-29ecd3857c43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121413} {:answer/id #uuid "1b849bf1-aca3-5500-815a-f40453b5ee74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121414} {:answer/id #uuid "433c083a-6dc5-5054-a669-603ea27d72af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121415} {:answer/id #uuid "67771eb8-4b20-550f-a702-ea3bc2c1b29f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121416}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b849bf1-aca3-5500-815a-f40453b5ee74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121414} :answer-field/id #uuid "51d1f568-ebd7-5909-9b1b-31baaaef0c6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121411}] :question/id #uuid "0151a70d-ce12-5172-8abb-638426f2b102" :question/math-academy-id "q-162747" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1945/Source/Images/q-162747.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z = f(x, y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local minimum points?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124740 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40d58ee6-1121-5ddc-a96f-1a7fa7a193dd" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Sylvester's Criterion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8765f10-c7b0-5ad7-a11c-eef46cd70f0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121460} {:answer/id #uuid "e6f6b933-2f0b-5e9d-83dd-b16685eb73ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121461} {:answer/id #uuid "f58312f3-62d8-5c58-96d2-817f5082bebb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121462} {:answer/id #uuid "024e0185-9671-5f4e-a5f4-c4fd83ddc179" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121463} {:answer/id #uuid "7da88191-9614-5886-b8af-c1d15ac2ad6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7da88191-9614-5886-b8af-c1d15ac2ad6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121464} :answer-field/id #uuid "75ae0d45-4a0c-54df-9bf8-4f480562c303" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121459}] :question/id #uuid "f4f668d6-a868-59b1-9e4d-3e52d3a52a67" :question/math-academy-id "q-163723" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x = a ∈ R^{3}$ is given by\n$f(x) \\approx - 9 + \\frac{1}{2}(x - a)^{T}A(x - a)$,\n\nwhere\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $a_{11} = 6$ and $det(A) > 0$\n2. $A$ is positive-definite\n3. $a$ is a local minimum of $f$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124741 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "233eab4c-ab17-53a8-ae03-f6f28ac78922" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e1b448c-77c1-53e9-9216-4b32e2b02bc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inconclusive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121466} {:answer/id #uuid "f89c515f-ce74-5c10-b07c-a7fec487a792" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121467} {:answer/id #uuid "09ed0428-f0f4-5d0b-817b-4de98252672d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "local minimum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121468} {:answer/id #uuid "156f2863-510b-58e7-b0ea-f145b46673da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "local maximum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121469} {:answer/id #uuid "3ca0470d-9920-5323-a3f4-2d35dc402008" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not a critical point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121470}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f89c515f-ce74-5c10-b07c-a7fec487a792" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121467} :answer-field/id #uuid "44aa8dd5-916d-5c5b-96e8-fbbe2448a88d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121465}] :question/id #uuid "a10a1d5a-8a2c-54e0-8fb3-61f7b8f41913" :question/math-academy-id "q-163787" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x = a ∈ R^{3}$ is given by\n$f(x) \\approx - 3 + \\frac{1}{2}(x - a)^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}(x - a)$.\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of $f^{″}(a)$ are $λ_{1} =-2, λ_{2} =-\\sqrt{6}$ and $λ_{3} = \\sqrt{6}$, what type of point is $a$?"}]}
